 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: traditional, printed 1888",
  "tune": "public domain: traditional, printed 1888 (S\u014dkyokush\u016b) and 1894 by Rudolf Dittrich (died 1919), from whose printing it is read",
  "arrangement": "original, written for Stackapella",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "melody and words from Rudolf Dittrich, Nippon Gakufu: Sechs japanische Volkslieder gesammelt und f\u00fcr das Klavier bearbeitet / Six Japanese Popular Songs (Leipzig: Breitkopf & H\u00e4rtel, plate 20313, undated, catalogued 1894; Dittrich, the only person the book names as collector or arranger, died 1919), No. 3, \"Sakura\", pp. 4 to 5: the melody, the top voice of his piano setting, in D minor, written here a whole tone lower, every pitch and rhythm his except that we rest on the first beat of bar 13 where his piano carries an F (in our key) into \"Izaya\"; the words, printed by him as verse above the music, word for word as sung here. Read from the Bayerische Staatsbibliothek's scan, 2 Mus.pr. 4236 (urn:nbn:de:bvb:12-bsb11462745-6, images 10 and 11). The fitting of the syllables to the notes, which Dittrich does not print, and the rest in bar 13 follow the S\u014dkyokush\u016b (Tokyo: Ministry of Education, 1888), No. 2, its earliest printing, consulted (dl.ndl.go.jp/pid/857651, frames 22 and 23; its preface names Isawa and eight selectors of words and music). The held notes at the ends of lines shortened for breaths, where Dittrich holds a half note"
